Door one is for meetings you''re sitting in. Before the call you type pai-meet record. Two things start recording at once: your microphone, so we capture your voice, and a virtual audio cable called BlackHole that catches what everyone else says coming out of your speakers. When the meeting ends you type pai-meet stop. It transcribes both tracks separately — and that gives a free bonus: because your mic is one track and their audio is another, we automatically know who said what, you versus them, with no guessing. It merges them in time order, writes the notes, and drops it all into a folder under Meetings named by date and title. Two commands, the rest is automatic.

Door two is for meetings you can''t attend. You hand the tool a meeting link. On your Hetzner server, the open-source bot Attendee joins the call as a visible participant — it literally shows up named something like Yaron''s notetaker, recording — and grabs the audio. We pull that file back and run it through the exact same engine. Same folder, same shape. The only difference between the doors is where the audio comes from; once we have audio, it''s identical.

How we build it, three steps. Step one, the big one: the engine as a command-line tool called pai-meet, sitting right next to your other tools like pai-books and pai-gmail, same pattern — transcribe, notes, record, stop, and storage. All local, nothing risky, I can build it now. Step two is a one-time bit of plumbing on your Mac: installing BlackHole and setting up a combined output so meeting audio goes to your headphones and the recorder at the same time — that needs an install and a reboot, so that part''s on you, but it''s a two-minute walk-through. Step three, later and on your say-so, is standing up the Attendee bot on your Hetzner box, since that touches your server.

So the order is: I build the engine and in-room recording first, you do the two-minute audio setup, we test on a real meeting, then we add the remote bot. Want me to start building the engine?','https://pub-6fcd8a707615437da46fb97b8be570ba.r2.dev/062bd372-ca70-42ef-a41c-ca442d0f65c6.mp3','REX — how it works + build plan',115,'message','am_onyx','2026-06-09T07:55:02.142Z') ON CONFLICT(id) DO NOTHING;

So how did we burn five gigabytes in days? Two things working together. The first was a polling loop. The tickets board re-fetched the entire ticket list every five seconds, automatically, the whole time the page was open — with no off switch when the tab went into the background. So even a dashboard tab you forgot about, sitting behind your other windows, kept hammering the database every five seconds. Each answer was about thirty-five kilobytes — tiny. But five seconds means seven hundred and twenty requests an hour, around twenty-five megabytes an hour, every hour the tab was alive. Leave one tab open around the clock and that''s roughly six hundred megabytes a day, doing nothing but pulling the same list thousands of times. A weekend of that on a device or two gets you most of the way to the cap.

The second drain was heavier in bursts: the voice audio. We stored the actual audio files as raw binary inside the database — about a hundred and thirty-five megabytes. And to play them, the app pulled them back out encoded as text, which inflates them by a third, closer to a hundred and eighty megabytes. A fresh browser re-pulls that whole backlog. So every cold open of the dashboard is a hundred and eighty megabytes of egress in one shot, just for the voice panel. A few cold loads and you''ve spent a gigabyte. Stack that on the constant polling and five gigabytes in a few days isn''t surprising — it''s the expected outcome.

I''ll be straight on the measurement: Neon''s per-query stats were off and we don''t have an API key, so I can''t hand you an exact percentage split. This is reasoned from byte math — but the math is solid.

Now R2. R2 is Cloudflare''s file storage, and its defining feature is that transfer out of it is free and uncapped — no egress limit at all. So when we move the voice audio out of the database into an R2 bucket, two good things happen. One: the database stops storing those heavy files — it only keeps a tiny text pointer, a few dozen bytes per clip instead of half a megabyte of binary. So the database shrinks from a hundred-plus megabytes down to a few. Two, the big one: when the browser plays a clip, it fetches it straight from R2, not from the database. That transfer never touches Neon''s quota, and R2 doesn''t meter it. The single heaviest source of egress just vanishes. Combine that with killing the polling loop, which is already done in the code, and the database goes from leaking gigabytes to sending a few small JSON responses. That''s the whole fix — and it''s why the platform was never the problem.','https://pub-6fcd8a707615437da46fb97b8be570ba.r2.dev/1550ab1b-3d71-4dd1-9058-573017abfcce.mp3','ATLAS | Why 5GB, and R2',189,'message','bm_george','2026-06-09T14:38:29.843Z') ON CONFLICT(id) DO NOTHING;

Now the voice. Today the broken way is that the MP3''s raw bytes are stored inside Neon, in a binary column, and playback reads them back out of the database — that''s the drain. After we move to R2, the audio file lives in R2, and the database keeps only a small metadata row: which agent spoke, the text, the timestamp, and a pointer — a URL to the R2 file. So to answer you directly: yes, something about each voice message is still reflected in the database, but it''s just lightweight text, not the sound. The sound itself is in Cloudflare R2. And the code already treats that audio URL as the single playable source — the migration note literally says swapping it to an R2 URL is invisible to the frontend. So we just change what the pointer points at; the player doesn''t change.

On creation: it genuinely doesn''t matter whether the voice is made locally by Kokoro on your Mac, which is what happens now, or by a Cloudflare Worker doing text-to-speech. Either way the output is the same — an MP3 that goes to R2, plus a small metadata record. Creation is decoupled from storage. Pick it on cost and quality, not on this.

And your real question — do we need Neon, or can it all be Cloudflare. For the voice flow specifically, it can be a hundred percent Cloudflare: R2 for the audio, a Worker, and D1, Cloudflare''s own SQLite database, for the metadata — never touching Neon. There''s a sliding scale. Level one, the fastest: audio to R2, voice metadata stays in Neon but it''s tiny now, basically free. Level two: voice fully on Cloudflare, metadata in D1, Neon untouched by voice. Level three: migrate the whole dashboard off Neon onto D1 plus R2 — no Neon at all, which is the full consolidation you said you wanted. The only reason Neon''s still in the picture is that the rest of the dashboard — projects, goals, tickets, skills — lives there today. Voice is the easy first piece to carve off. So the real question for you is just how far off Neon you want to go: voice only, or the whole thing.','https://pub-6fcd8a707615437da46fb97b8be570ba.r2.dev/47baff73-ee5f-4f4a-b455-52494b3cb1ca.mp3','ATLAS | Voice flow + Cloudflare',139,'message','bm_george','2026-06-09T14:51:15.769Z') ON CONFLICT(id) DO NOTHING;

Now, why are you on Neon at all? The honest answer is you didn''t choose it — you inherited it. This dashboard is a clone of Erez''s template, and that template was built on Neon Postgres. The clone just kept the data layer. The split isn''t a decision anyone made for your dashboard''s needs; it''s the template''s architecture carried over. And the template choosing Postgres was reasonable for a template — Postgres is the default serious database, a template meant to host many tenants wants that headroom, and Neon specifically solved the awkward part of running Postgres on serverless with its HTTP driver. Good reasons for a multi-tenant SaaS template. Almost none of them apply to your single-tenant copy of it.

So then: what''s special about Neon that we can''t do in D1? I censused every migration, and for your schema the answer is essentially nothing. You use JSON blobs, UUID columns, timestamps, and upserts — and every one of those has a direct SQLite equivalent. What you do not use is the stuff that would actually lock you to Postgres: no vector search, no full-text search, no geographic data, no exotic extensions. The only Postgres extension in there is pgcrypto, and that''s just to generate UUIDs, which the Worker can do itself. So nothing in this dashboard depends on a capability D1 lacks.

To be fair, there are real places Postgres beats D1 — semantic vector search, heavy concurrent writers, raw maturity, and portability if you ever leave Cloudflare. But you have one writer, which is you, a few megabytes of data, and you''ve already decided to consolidate on Cloudflare. So all of those advantages are theoretical for you.

Which means the split is pure liability for your case: two providers, two bills, two quotas, two failure domains, and a cross-cloud hop on every query — the very thing that broke it. Moving to D1 plus R2 collapses all of that into one platform with no metered egress. The cost of doing it is real but it''s grind, not a wall — translating the SQL dialect, swapping the query API, and moving UUID and timestamp defaults into the Worker. The one thing I''d check before calling it easy is whether any query filters inside a JSON field rather than just storing and returning it whole. Everything else is mechanical.','https://pub-6fcd8a707615437da46fb97b8be570ba.r2.dev/0608e629-9e65-4a90-abd1-f0e046de383b.mp3','ATLAS | Why Neon vs D1',177,'message','bm_george','2026-06-09T14:57:49.316Z') ON CONFLICT(id) DO NOTHING;
